          "secNumber": "4.2",
          "heading": "Crash-fault consensus through Raft and Paxos ideas",
          "content": "Raft is a consensus algorithm designed around leader election, log replication, and safety constraints. Each node is commonly a follower, candidate, or leader for a given term. A candidate solicits votes; a leader appends log entries and sends append requests; followers enforce prefix matching and term rules. A client command becomes committed only under the algorithm\u2019s commitment conditions, not merely because the leader appended it locally. A leader must observe the special Raft rule that prevents it from deciding earlier-term entries solely by counting replicas; it commits an entry from its current term using an appropriate majority before relying on commitment of preceding entries. This subtle condition is often omitted from educational shortcuts. Correctness arguments also rely on the voter restriction that a candidate's log is sufficiently up to date relative to voters.\n\nTake three nodes A, B, C. A is leader in term 5 and has appended an entry at index 10 on A and B. The implementation may commit when the defined conditions hold. Now imagine A loses communication before confirming a different entry at index 11. C becomes a candidate in term 6 and requests votes. Voters compare terms and log freshness before granting them; a new leader is not permitted to discard a previously committed entry. During reconciliation, conflicting uncommitted suffixes can be overwritten. The visual distinction between commit index and last log index is essential. A student who interprets the longest log as 'all entries confirmed' will misdiagnose failures. Some log entries are speculative until their commitment conditions hold, and different nodes may temporarily have differing suffixes.\n\nPaxos expresses similar agreement requirements through ballots and durable promises, but its organization differs from Raft's explicit leader-log narrative. In single-decree Paxos, a proposer obtains promises from a quorum, may need to carry forward the highest previously accepted value, and then asks acceptors to accept a proposal. Multi-Paxos applies repeated decisions under a stable leader optimization. Neither description means all implementations are interchangeable: membership, snapshotting, transaction routing, and durability design still matter. Students should compare the two in terms of invariants and information retained across failures, rather than claiming one is inherently faster in every environment. Raft's understandable exposition does not free implementers from verifying real-world variants.\n\nOperationally, a three-voter group can tolerate one voting-node crash for continued progress when communication between the remaining two is sufficient. A five-voter group can tolerate two, but each write may interact with a larger set of hosts. Learners should not mistake higher replica count for linearly improved durability when all replicas share a faulty storage domain. Observer replicas can serve reads or backups depending on the system but do not count as voters unless configured as such. Monitoring should expose leader term, commit index, applied index, replication lag, and rejected stale requests. An election followed by a prolonged apply backlog is different from an election that cannot find a majority. Both may appear as slow client writes if only front-end response time is measured.\n\nBuild a simplified Raft log explorer with three voters and an explicit current-term commit rule. Controls allow append command, deliver follower acknowledgement, simulate crash, start election, and reconcile logs. Draw three distinct marks for appended, majority replicated, and committed. Attempt to construct two different committed values at the same log index; a correct test harness should reject every such trace under its specified transitions. Add an incorrectly implemented follower that forgets its persisted vote after restart; the lab can then display the violated assumption. Separately compare message counts during stable leadership and during repeated elections. State visibly that the model excludes joint configuration changes, log compaction, disk torn writes, and adversarial scheduling that is beyond the finite traces exercised.",
          "simulations": []
