          "secNumber": "4.5",
          "heading": "Capstone: consensus under a regional network split",
          "content": "Design a replicated metadata log for a three-region science data portal. The log records which storage shard owns a dataset identifier, which model version is active, and which researchers have publishing permission. Contradictory authoritative metadata could route a write to the wrong owner or expose a publication before review. Place one voter in each region for the first design and document which regional failures the configuration is expected to tolerate. With three voters, two are required for a majority. A partition isolating one region leaves the other two able to form a quorum if their links and processes are healthy, while the isolated voter cannot establish new authoritative decisions. The system may still answer explicitly stale metadata queries from cache, but it must not pretend those are fresh authority checks.\n\nA log proposal is distinct from a committed update. Let term 12 leader A append 'owner(dataset-7)=shard-X' at index 120 to itself and one follower. If the actual protocol's current-term quorum commit conditions hold, the entry becomes committed. A can crash before notifying the client, creating an ambiguous response, but a future legitimate leader must retain the entry. The test harness should record each node's term, vote, log prefix, commit index, and applied index to reconstruct the outcome. The next leader might have a longer local uncommitted suffix, yet voting restrictions ensure it does not undermine committed history. Do not assert safety solely from leader identity or the highest numerical term. The log matching and commitment rules establish which history is authoritative.\n\nNow create an adversarial schedule in which two candidates start elections almost simultaneously. Delay their requests so votes split and neither reaches a majority. After randomized election backoff and eventually stable communication, one can usually become leader and restore progress. Under a fully asynchronous scheduler with unlimited delay there is no guaranteed termination for deterministic consensus with a possible crash. The capstone should therefore state safety as unconditional under its model and progress as depending on eventual favorable conditions. Students need to observe that a failed election is not itself a safety violation. A temporary period without a leader may be the correct price for refusing contradictory updates.\n\nThe design changes when a fourth region is added. A new host should not be made a voter by editing one local configuration file at a time; incompatible configurations may create unsafe quorum interpretations. The service needs a rigorous reconfiguration protocol that transitions ownership of voting authority safely. Read-only learners can be added with a different risk profile, because they are not automatically part of the commit quorum. For administrators, document how to remove a dead voter without creating overlapping membership histories. The maintenance runbook should show an authorized reconfiguration path and a recovery route from operator mistakes. Even perfect consensus code can fail its organizational objective if the only operator with control-plane permissions is unreachable during an outage.\n\nA verification plan combines trace exploration with observable effects. Enumerate partitions, crash points, message reorderings, restarts, and delayed acknowledgements. For each trace test whether two different values were committed at the same index, whether any committed entry disappeared after election, and whether clients were told 'confirmed' before the commit condition held. Inspect liveness only for traces in which the required majority eventually communicates. Add state-machine commands that carry stable operation IDs so retrying an ambiguous metadata update cannot create a second logical action. This does not prove all possible executions; model checking a bounded state space can give stronger evidence for selected safety properties. The report should say exactly which failure modes were excluded, such as Byzantine voters or torn disk writes.",
