          "secNumber": "7.2",
          "heading": "Fault injection and chaos experiments",
          "content": "Fault injection deliberately introduces bounded failures to test whether a system behaves according to its stated recovery and safety properties. A 'chaos experiment' is useful only when it has a hypothesis, blast-radius limit, abort condition, and measurements. Randomly killing servers without specifying expected behavior can provide excitement but little reliable learning. Begin with a steady-state claim such as 'confirmed writes remain unique during one voting-node crash, although p95 latency may increase'. Select a failure that challenges a known assumption: network partition, delayed storage acknowledgement, process restart, high packet loss, disk-full error, or stale DNS cache. Define how the experiment stops if it threatens unrelated users or violates a critical safety invariant. Fault injection in a production environment also requires appropriate authorization and rollback planning.\n\nA failure should be traced through dependency graphs, not tested in isolation. Killing one API replica may leave the service healthy behind a load balancer, but killing a shared identity dependency can make all replicas reject new clients. Adding 300 milliseconds of inter-zone latency may cause a consensus group to elect leaders repeatedly if its timeout design is too aggressive. Increasing message drops may cause retries to amplify downstream load. These secondary effects are the educational value of well-instrumented fault injection. Inspect client-facing error and latency distributions, queue depth, election frequency, and data-integrity checks before and during the experiment. A test that reports all hosts alive while violating seat-count invariants should fail by correctness criteria.\n\nConsider a three-voter library reservation service. The hypothesis is that losing voter C will not create duplicate confirmed reservations, and that A and B can continue once a valid leader is available. The team injects C's crash and submits a sequence of requests carrying unique idempotency keys. It checks both client responses and the final durable allocation state. Next it injects a partition isolating the current leader A from B and C. The expectation changes: A should refuse or redirect authoritative writes after it cannot meet quorum. If operators see A still returning success while B and C elect a new leader, the test has uncovered a potentially serious safety defect. Simply counting HTTP 200 responses would have hidden the problem.\n\nExperiments need repeatability and scope. Store the random seed, start and end time, topology version, injected faults, software revision, and relevant configuration. Repeat a small laboratory trace before attempting a larger staging run. Realistic tests may require control-plane failures, credential expiration, or unavailable backups, but those should be staged with stricter safeguards because recovery may be difficult. A failure observed once may be timing-dependent; interpret negative findings carefully. Passing a finite suite does not mathematically prove safety under every possible message ordering. Combine fault injection with property-based testing, model checking where practical, and rigorous review of critical protocol transitions.\n\nBuild an experiment planner that lets the learner choose a hypothesis, one crash, one partition, or an acknowledgement delay. The tool records the expected invariant and a set of observables. Run the same synthetic client trace with and without the fault, comparing confirmed reservation uniqueness and time to restore service. Include a stop button that halts further injections but preserves the trace for diagnosis. Require students to write which failure conditions they did not test and what evidence would justify escalating from isolated local testing to a controlled staging experiment. The exercise is intentionally safe and local; it cannot actually partition live networks or shut down cloud instances.",
          "simulations": []
