          "secNumber": "7.5",
          "heading": "Capstone: incident review of a cascading retry storm",
          "content": "A course-submission service experiences rising timeout errors during a busy examination window. The first symptom is a surge in gateway p99 latency, although the core database remains operational. Logs reveal that the inventory-style validation service began returning intermittent 503 responses following a routine leader election. Each client retries three times, each gateway retries a further three times, and the downstream adapter retries again. The observed rate reaching the database is several times the rate of original student submissions. This feedback saturates connection pools, causing previously healthy operations to time out and retry. The incident is not best understood as three independent services failing. It is a dependency graph in which a modest initial disturbance was amplified by inconsistent retry policy.\n\nReconstruct one user journey with an operation ID. Student U pressed Submit once at 10:05:12. A first attempt reached the backend and committed the submission, but the response was delayed beyond the gateway deadline. A later attempt retried with a newly generated key and caused another grade row; the UI displayed an error after all attempts exceeded their deadlines. This is simultaneously a latency incident and a data-correctness incident. An alert on HTTP error rate alone would not identify the duplicate grade. The response team should correlate API attempts with durable business effects, check uniqueness constraints, and stop unsafe retries before attempting large-scale autoscaling. The first corrective measure may be to pause redundant retries and enforce a stable submission key, not to add more capacity.\n\nA good incident timeline includes exact changes and uncertainty. At 10:04:58 a leader term advanced, at 10:05:02 queue wait increased, at 10:05:05 retries exceeded originals, and at 10:05:09 rejected connections increased. If tracing was sampled, the team should label gaps instead of inventing missing spans. Examine configuration history to learn whether timeouts changed recently, but do not assume causality based solely on temporal proximity. Check whether the incident coincided with peak traffic and whether server resource limits had already been near saturation. Separate the initiating event from the amplification mechanism and from the missing idempotency guard that allowed duplicate effects. Each requires a different preventive action and a different test.\n\nThe recovery plan introduces one retry owner, bounded exponential backoff with jitter, a propagated overall deadline, and load-shedding rules. The payment or grading sink uses a durable operation ledger to return one result for each stable key. The worker queue has a finite size and rejects overflow quickly, avoiding memory exhaustion. Observability adds dashboards showing original operations, attempted RPCs, committed effects, and deduplication hits separately. The team tests a leader election under load in staging and confirms that retries do not create an unstable feedback loop. Changing the timeout alone might mask one symptom while leaving duplicate-effect risk unresolved; therefore corrective tests must include both performance and safety assertions.\n\nClose with a blameless evidence review that still assigns concrete ownership. Which team owns the idempotency contract? Who can change retry defaults shared across SDKs? Who maintains database uniqueness and a repair procedure for duplicate grades? Which alarm should have fired when attempted work grew faster than original demand? Write explicit acceptance conditions: no duplicate committed submission for one operation ID, a bounded ratio of RPC attempts to originals under an injected partial outage, and a documented recovery of ambiguous outcomes. A finite load test cannot establish permanent safety, but it provides a repeatable barrier against regression. Present unresolved risks honestly, including long-delayed retries after key expiration and dependencies without external idempotency support.",
