          "secNumber": "8.5",
          "heading": "Capstone: choose an architecture for a global STEM platform",
          "content": "A global STEM platform serves static textbooks, interactive simulations, discussion activity, library search, and a limited number of authenticated contribution workflows. These functions have sharply different consistency and availability needs. Public textbook pages are immutable between releases and benefit from static hosting and caching. Simulation code runs locally in the reader and should not require a live network for every slider change. Discussions and author submissions involve mutable state, moderation, authentication and notifications. Before dividing this product into microservices, write a traffic and risk profile for each feature. A small team may operate a static frontend and one well-structured backend more reliably than a fleet of loosely coordinated services. Distribution should solve measured constraints, not create the appearance of sophistication.\n\nBegin with the reader experience. Chapter routes should have stable canonical URLs and server-visible content so users can access them under limited connectivity and search engines can find substantive teaching material. Client simulations need bounded input controls and should distinguish pedagogical models from physically complete experiments. The content deployment process is an atomic release problem: a navigation index, chapter pages, and assets need compatible versions. Publishing an updated index before its referenced chapter pages are available creates broken links even though every individual file is syntactically valid. A release manifest with content hashes and verified staging routes improves confidence. Keep drafts out of public sitemaps, and preserve previous approved material when adding a new subject.\n\nFor authenticated contributions, one durable state store can hold submission status and idempotency keys. A contributor uploads an article, a validation worker checks metadata and file safety, and a moderator decides whether to publish. These stages need not share one synchronous HTTP request. Store the initial submission transaction with a stable operation ID, then use an outbox or equivalent mechanism to schedule asynchronous validation. The site can display RECEIVED, CHECKING, NEEDS_REVISION, APPROVED, or REJECTED states. A failed notification service should not erase a submitted manuscript. The moderation decision needs authorization and an audit record; a worker that merely processed a file should not have the right to publish it to all readers. This boundary reflects the actual domain more clearly than a generic 'content microservice'.\n\nNow consider worldwide deployment. A CDN can serve most public reading traffic from nearby points of presence, reducing origin load. A central authoritative write region may suffice for low-volume submissions; replication and disaster recovery protect against major outages. Introducing globally writable databases and cross-region consensus for a handful of daily submissions would add cost and complexity without much benefit. If future demand justifies regional authoring, redesign with explicit conflict and ownership semantics. Keep data residency, backup retention, account deletion, and trace privacy in the risk assessment. Availability objectives should distinguish browsing a cached textbook from accepting a new research submission. The platform can honestly degrade to read-only mode during a write-service outage rather than show false success.\n\nThe final architecture review weighs alternatives. Option A is a static reader plus a modular backend with a transactional database and managed job queue. Option B splits discussion, contribution, search indexing, and moderation into separately deployable services. Compare each option's critical dependencies, expected traffic, operational burden, recovery time, and risk of duplicate or lost events. Stress-test a simulated contribution when the worker crashes after validation but before acknowledgement; confirm that retry uses the same operation key. Test a release with one missing chapter asset and require the deployment gate to fail before exposure. The best design is the smallest one that meets documented user and correctness requirements while remaining operable by the actual team. Architectural complexity should be earned by evidence from workload and incidents.",
